__pycache__/
*.pyc

# pbrun writes these into whatever tree it submits from.  They are ignored
# here, in the tracked file, rather than in one checkout's .git/info/exclude,
# because the damage is done by whoever's checkout lacks the rule: an
# untracked stamp is filtered out of the action key by name (pbrun.py's
# _git_identity), but a *committed* one is not -- `git diff HEAD` is appended
# to the digest unfiltered, so a tracked stamp moves the key on every submit,
# which is the "cache miss dressed up as a different action" that filter
# exists to prevent.  One `git add -A` from a shared checkout re-opens it.
#
# The patterns are the exact grammar `core.pbrun_git_exclude_patterns` defines
# -- a fixed prefix, sixteen hex characters, a fixed suffix -- and not the
# broad globs that used to stand in for them.  `pbrun` writes these same two
# lines into every checkout's `.git/info/exclude`, and replaces the broad ones
# when it finds them, because `pbrun_result.*` also matches a repository file
# somebody named `pbrun_result.notes.py`.  A tracked file dropped by an ignore
# rule is a bundle that does not rebuild the tree the action ran in.
.pbrun-closure.[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].json
pbrun_result.[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].txt
